          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is osteoarthritis not simply “wear and tear” of a joint?",
          "answer": "Osteoarthritis is not just a joint wearing out. The general clinical reference on osteoarthritis describes it as a disorder of the whole joint, including the cartilage, the bone underneath, the ligaments, the joint lining and the surrounding muscle. It says cartilage is progressively lost and the bone beneath it remodels in response. The same source calls the process \"an active one involving inflammation and attempted repair, not simple mechanical erosion.\" That is why the \"wear and tear\" idea is considered inaccurate.\n\nThe sources I searched do not describe the specific inflammatory or bone-remodelling mechanisms in any detail, so I can't explain them further from this material. A clinician or a dedicated rheumatology resource would be the place to ask.\n\nThe reference also lists risk factors beyond mechanical load:\n- Age\n- Previous joint injury or surgery, which is the main factor in young people with knee osteoarthritis\n- Obesity, which acts both mechanically and metabolically\n- Joint shape and alignment\n- Muscle weakness\n- Genetic factors\n- Female sex, particularly for hand and knee disease after menopause\n\nBecause the same source says muscle weakness from reduced use can make the joint worse, it recommends structured exercise as the core treatment. The guidance also notes that the condition is manageable over long periods and does not inevitably lead to joint replacement.\n\nIf you have joint pain, a doctor can assess it. This is general information and not a diagnosis.\n\nSources: \"Osteoarthritis\" (general clinical reference); \"Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options\" (rheumatology and orthopaedic society guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
